    {
      "@context": "https://schema.org",
      "@type": "MusicEvent",
      "id": "33d3c3e64c6d483c",
      "title": "Ομιλητής: Μιχάλης Κοσμόπουλος",
      "description": "Ομιλητής: Μιχάλης Κοσμόπουλος",
      "fullDescription": "You settle into the lecture hall at Megaron Mousikis and the academic atmosphere is palpable. This isn't performance but discourse — a space where ideas get examined in real time, where expertise meets curious audience. Michalis Kosmopoulos takes the podium with something to say.\n\nThe Megaron hosts lecture series that complement its performance calendar, offering intellectual engagement with topics that touch the arts, history, and Greek culture. Kosmopoulos speaks from a position of authority in his field, bringing specialized knowledge to an audience of engaged listeners. The format allows for depth that popular presentations sacrifice.\n\nThe crowd for Megaron lectures differs from concert audiences. You'll find academics and students, yes, but also educated general audiences who seek substantive discourse. Notebooks appear. Questions form behind attentive eyes. The silence is the productive kind — active listening rather than passive attendance. When Kosmopoulos makes a particular point, you can feel heads tilting in recognition or resistance.\n\nThe lecture halls at Megaron provide the technical infrastructure these presentations require — good acoustics, comfortable seating for extended attention, projection capabilities for visual materials. The architecture suggests seriousness of purpose without being oppressive. You're in a space designed for ideas to circulate.\n\nIf you need entertainment and spectacle, if you want passive consumption, a Megaron lecture requires too much of you. These events assume an audience willing to follow complex arguments, to sit with difficulty, to let ideas accumulate. But if you're hungry for discourse that respects your intelligence, for expertise shared generously with those prepared to receive it — this is how Athens does public intellect.\n\n| Info | Details |\n|------|---------|\n| **Date** | Friday, February 27, 2026 |\n| **Time** | 19:00 |\n| **Venue** | Megaron Mousikis, Vasilissis Sofias & Kokkali |\n| **Getting there** | Megaro Moussikis Metro (Blue line), direct access |\n| **Price** | Often free with registration; check program |\n\nWhen Megaron shifts from performance to ideas, the audience leans forward instead of back.",
      "startDate": "2026-02-27T19:00:00+03:00",
      "endDate": null,
      "type": "concert",
